mud-hotak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-hotak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Hotak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Hotak was an Afghan ruler of the Ghilzai Hotak dynasty, son of Mirwais Hotak.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud II was Ottoman sultan from 1808 to 1839.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii-seljuk-sultan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-ii-seljuk-sultan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud II (Seljuk sultan)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud II was Seljuk sultan of the western lands, centred on Iraq and western Iran, from 1118 to 1131.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-iv"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud IV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud IV was the last mansa of the Mali Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-khalji.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Khalji</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Khalji was Sultan of Malwa from 1436 to 1469 and founder of its Khalji dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud of Ghazni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud of Ghazni was ruler of the Ghaznavid Empire from 998 to 1030, from his capital at Ghazni in Afghanistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-bahmani"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Shah Bahmani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Shah was sultan of the Bahmani Sultanate from 1482 to 1518, coming to the throne as a boy after the death of Muhammad Shah III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-durrani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-shah-durrani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Shah Durrani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Shah Durrani was a Sadozai ruler of the Durrani Empire, reigning in Kabul from 1801 to 1803 and again from 1809 to 1818.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-ii-of-johor"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Shah II of Johor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Shah II was sultan of Johor from 1685 to 1699 and the last ruler descended from the sultans of Malacca.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-ii-of-malwa"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Shah II of Malwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Shah II was the last sultan of the Khalji dynasty of Malwa, reigning from 1511 to 1531.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-iii-of-johor"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Shah III of Johor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Shah III was sultan of Johor and Riau from 1761 to 1812, succeeding as a child.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-of-malacca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahmud-shah-of-malacca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Shah of Malacca</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Shah of Malacca was the last sultan of Malacca, ruling from 1488 until the Portuguese under Afonso de Albuquerque captured the city in 1511.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-yalavach"><span class="enc-name">Mahmud Yalavach</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahmud Yalavach was a Khwarazmian Muslim administrator in the service of the Mongol Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahoba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahoba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahoba</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahoba is a town in Bundelkhand, in southern Uttar Pradesh, India, and the headquarters of the district of the same name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahodayapuram.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahodayapuram</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahodayapuram, also called Makotai, was the capital of the Chera Perumals of Kerala from about the 9th to the early 12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mahón</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mahón, or Maó, is the capital of Menorca in the Balearic Islands, Spain, set at the head of one of the largest natural harbours in the Mediterranean.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mai-title"><span class="enc-name">Mai (title)</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Mai was the title of the